1
00:00:01,420 --> 00:00:03,440
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:04,120
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:13,820
My name is Catherine and today we have an intermediate level lesson all about a very special kind of person who helps you out with your kids.

4
00:00:14,260 --> 00:00:14,600
That's right.

5
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:16,480
Today we want to get a nanny.

6
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:16,680
A nanny.

7
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:17,600
A nanny.

8
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:22,980
So, a nanny is a person, usually a woman, but it can be a man as well I guess.

9
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:27,250
And this person takes care of your children when you're working or when you're out of the house.

10
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:28,880
That's right.

11
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:29,810
So, this is what's going on today.

12
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:33,830
We're going to see what type of responsibilities a nanny has.

13
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:37,050
And so, let's listen to her dialogue and we'll be back in a bit.

14
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:45,760
Hey Mel, are you up for some tennis today?

15
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:47,170
Sorry, I can't.

16
00:00:47,570 --> 00:00:54,570
I have to go to work, pick up Jake and Maddie from school, and make them an afternoon snack, then take Jake to soccer practice and Maddie to dance class.

17
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:56,710
You sound exhausted.

18
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:00,240
Maybe you should hire a nanny to help you out.

19
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:03,350
She can pick the kids up and take them to their after school activities.

20
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:07,530
She can also help you do some household chores and run some errands.

21
00:01:07,910 --> 00:01:08,890
Oh, I don't know.

22
00:01:09,510 --> 00:01:10,810
It's hard to find the right nanny.

23
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:16,990
You have to consider her previous work experience, the responsibilities you give her, and how she interacts with the kids.

24
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:19,350
I would love to have someone to help me out though.

25
00:01:19,670 --> 00:01:21,590
I think you should definitely consider it.

26
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:27,710
This way you won't have to juggle such a busy schedule and you'll still get to spend time with the kids in the evenings.

27
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:30,940
I can refer you to this great nanny, Amy.

28
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:34,100
She used to work for my neighbors before they moved away.

29
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,160
She's very responsible, a good cook, and great with kids.

30
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:40,600
Oh, that's great.

31
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:40,820
Thanks Grace.

32
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:41,780
Can you give me her number?

33
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:43,920
I'll talk it over with Julie and give her a call tomorrow.

34
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:49,280
Maybe this way I won't be so tired every day and Julie and I might even get to go on a date once in a while.

35
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:54,400
Alright, we're back.

36
00:01:54,420 --> 00:01:57,440
So now let's take a look at some key words on Language Takeaway.

37
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:00,480
Language Takeaway.

38
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:06,880
So as you said, Marco, we don't just find a nanny, we hire a nanny.

39
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:08,760
So what does the word hire mean?

40
00:02:08,890 --> 00:02:17,870
Okay, so we have the verb to hire someone and that means to get someone to work for you and you pay them obviously money in exchange for their services.

41
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:25,090
Okay, so this is different from finding a job for someone because I could find a job for someone but maybe that job is at a restaurant.

42
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:31,000
But when I hire someone, that means I want that person to work for me or for my company.

43
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:31,180
That's right.

44
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
So in this case, obviously we are not a company, we are just a couple of people who are looking for a nanny.

45
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:39,840
So we need to hire a nanny.

46
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:43,420
We need to get her to work for us and obviously you would pay her a salary.

47
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:49,440
Alright so when you pay her a salary, you also have some expectations about what the nanny will do.

48
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:54,760
So one of those expectations is that the nanny will take the children to their after school activities.

49
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:55,760
That's right.

50
00:02:55,820 --> 00:03:01,710
So this is a phrase if you will or a type of thing that happens after school.

51
00:03:01,950 --> 00:03:09,110
So usually children, when they finish their classes, they have some things that they can do at school like for example sports.

52
00:03:09,770 --> 00:03:10,110
That's right.

53
00:03:10,290 --> 00:03:14,010
So this is very common in America but not so common in other parts of the world.

54
00:03:14,370 --> 00:03:24,650
So just to explain, if your child is in high school or middle school, after school is over, there's no more class, your child might want to play soccer or to go to art class.

55
00:03:25,030 --> 00:03:31,030
And these are things that you do at the school but it's usually considered an activity, not a class.

56
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:33,270
So we call these after school activities.

57
00:03:33,570 --> 00:03:33,850
That's right.

58
00:03:34,090 --> 00:03:37,250
So these activities are usually for recreation.

59
00:03:38,170 --> 00:03:40,590
They're not really part of the curriculum.

60
00:03:40,910 --> 00:03:41,970
It's not a class.

61
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:44,600
No, you don't get marks or grades for these.

62
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:45,270
These are just for fun.

63
00:03:45,910 --> 00:03:52,650
A lot of people work and so they need to have these activities for their kids to stay at school until they finish working.

64
00:03:52,890 --> 00:03:53,350
That's right.

65
00:03:54,160 --> 00:04:05,280
Okay, so we want the nanny to take the children or pick them up from their after school activities and we also need the nanny to help out with some household chores.

66
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:08,310
Okay, so you should know where these chores are being done.

67
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:11,880
The first word is household which means in the house.

68
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:18,050
Okay, so examples of household chores would be like cleaning the windows.

69
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:19,550
Washing the dishes.

70
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:21,010
Or doing laundry.

71
00:04:21,880 --> 00:04:26,000
So basically any type of cleaning around the house.

72
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,310
That's right, making the house look nicer.

73
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:30,600
And so usually children have chores.

74
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:35,080
These are things they need to do but a nanny can also help with household chores.

75
00:04:35,170 --> 00:04:39,630
Okay, and also we want the nanny to help and run some errands.

76
00:04:40,090 --> 00:04:40,930
Okay, so this is funny.

77
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:47,830
This phrasal, this phrase uses the word to run but we're not running like a sport.

78
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:52,110
We're going to do some errands but the verb here is to run errands.

79
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:55,130
So Marco, on Sunday I have to run some errands.

80
00:04:55,290 --> 00:04:57,290
I'm sorry I can't go out to the movie with you guys.

81
00:04:57,530 --> 00:05:00,310
Okay, so to run errands, what does that mean?

82
00:05:00,670 --> 00:05:06,790
Well an errand is like a job or a task that you have to do but it's usually not something you get paid for.

83
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:15,240
To run an errand is like to go to the grocery store and pick up food or to pick up your clothes at the laundromat.

84
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:17,200
You have dry cleaning.

85
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:21,620
Okay so they're kind of like chores I guess but you don't really do them in the house.

86
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:23,980
You have to go outside and run these errands.

87
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:26,480
You have to go pay your bills for example.

88
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:32,880
Exactly and so when I was growing up, on the weekends my mother would always run all of her errands because during the week she had to work.

89
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:34,300
Okay, very good.

90
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:36,040
So a lot of interesting words there.

91
