114
00:07:05,120 --> 00:07:05,360
Fluency Builder.

115
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:14,620
So this first phrase is a very spoken English phrase, something that you're going to hear all the time, especially if you're talking to your American friends.

116
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:18,040
We say, are you up for some tennis today?

117
00:07:18,300 --> 00:07:19,120
To be up for.

118
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:21,980
Okay, so to be up for something.

119
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:27,220
This is from the very first sentence when Grace says, hey Mel, are you up for some tennis today?

120
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:30,960
What does it mean if I ask someone if they are up for some tennis?

121
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:33,520
This means, would you like to play tennis today?

122
00:07:33,780 --> 00:07:35,640
Are you interested in playing tennis today?

123
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:37,580
So is this a good idea for you?

124
00:07:37,720 --> 00:07:38,440
Do you want to do this?

125
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:41,160
So basically, are you willing to play tennis today?

126
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:41,600
Yes.

127
00:07:42,560 --> 00:07:46,080
Alright, so to be up for is to be interested in or willing to do something.

128
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:47,740
Are you up for a movie tonight?

129
00:07:48,320 --> 00:07:54,140
Okay, or maybe you can ask your friend, are you up for grabbing a bite to eat later on?

130
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:54,940
Absolutely, I'm hungry.

131
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:58,660
So to be up for is a good way to ask a question.

132
00:07:58,660 --> 00:08:00,140
After that though, we have another phrase.

133
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:03,100
This phrase is very important when you're trying to get someone to help you.

134
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:04,760
So how do we ask, can you help me?

135
00:08:05,220 --> 00:08:12,500
In the dialogue we saw how Mel said that it would be a great idea to get a nanny because she would love to have someone to help me out.

136
00:08:13,140 --> 00:08:16,120
So to help someone out, to help me out.

137
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:19,960
So this is interesting because you could say, I need someone to help me.

138
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:23,720
But it's more common to hear the phrase, to help me out.

139
00:08:24,280 --> 00:08:25,580
So you're not going out.

140
00:08:26,700 --> 00:08:27,920
It doesn't have to do with space.

141
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,160
But we just say this phrase, to help out, means to help someone.

142
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:35,620
So it's basically the exact same thing, but it just sounds a lot more natural.

143
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:37,160
Exactly.

144
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:39,160
So, Marco, I'm moving tomorrow.

145
00:08:39,160 --> 00:08:40,540
Do you have some time to help me out?

146
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:44,760
Okay, so you say help me out, but you could also say, do you have time to help me?

147
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:46,530
And both are okay.

148
00:08:47,140 --> 00:08:50,250
Or if I'm talking about me and my friend, we're really busy tomorrow.

149
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:52,430
Do you have any time to help us out?

150
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:54,860
Okay, so we just changed the person there.

151
00:08:54,860 --> 00:08:55,710
We went to the plural form.

152
00:08:56,360 --> 00:08:58,350
Me, us, you.

153
00:08:59,230 --> 00:08:59,770
Okay, very good.

154
00:09:01,280 --> 00:09:06,320
And so for our last phrase, this is actually also very interesting.

155
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:14,420
When Grace was telling Mel that she's really busy and if she gets a nanny, she wouldn't have to juggle such a busy schedule.

156
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:17,560
All right, so the key verb here is to juggle.

157
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:22,520
Now I wish I could show this to you guys, but as this is an audio podcast, I can't.

158
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:24,540
What does the verb to juggle mean, Marco?

159
00:09:24,820 --> 00:09:37,530
Well, I don't know if you've ever seen clowns or at a circus where people have three balls and they throw them in the air with two hands and they're basically throwing stuff in the air, sometimes balls or pins and stuff like that.

160
00:09:37,590 --> 00:09:38,110
Sometimes fire.

161
00:09:38,410 --> 00:09:38,770
Fire.

162
00:09:38,990 --> 00:09:45,730
So that's to juggle, basically to have many things in your hands and be able to move them at the same time.

163
00:09:46,290 --> 00:09:51,070
All right, so when you're talking about a busy schedule, this means that you have lots and lots of things to do.

164
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:55,010
It's important to be able to juggle your busy schedule.

165
00:09:55,280 --> 00:09:58,830
That means to handle doing many things at the same time.

166
00:09:58,990 --> 00:09:59,510
That's right.

167
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:10,240
All right, so another way to say this is because you're so busy right now, having a nanny will help you complete your tasks or finish what you need to do.

168
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:10,470
That's right.

169
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:16,390
So you can also say the CEO of the company has to juggle a very busy schedule.

170
00:10:16,670 --> 00:10:19,250
All right, he has many, many things to do in general.

171
00:10:19,490 --> 00:10:19,850
That's right.

172
00:10:20,990 --> 00:10:21,990
So a lot of stuff.

173
